**Sector context**
Singapore manufacturing is structurally exposed to global trade cycles, with electronics, precision engineering, chemicals and biomedical clusters forming the core. Well-known trends include ongoing supply-chain diversification across ASEAN, rising capital intensity through automation and advanced manufacturing, and increasing compliance expectations around ESG disclosure. The sector also faces structural labour constraints, with an ageing workforce and reliance on foreign labour, making productivity gains central to long-term competitiveness. Global tendencies—friend-shoring, semiconductor cycle volatility and energy transition-related demand shifts—shape the sector's risk profile independent of the indicators above.

